Here's how security deposit rules compare in the three states, based on PropInspect's reviewed guides.

| | **California** | **Nevada** | **Texas** |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Deposit cap** | 1 month's rent. Some small landlords can charge up to 2 months, but never for active-duty service members. | 3 months' rent. This covers all security combined, including prepaid last month's rent. | No limit in state law |
| **Return deadline** | 21 days after the tenant moves out and hands back the unit | 30 days after the tenancy ends and the tenant hands back the unit, for either the refund or an itemized list of deductions | 30 days after the tenant hands back the unit. The refund depends on the tenant giving a forwarding address. |
| **Penalty for wrongly keeping the deposit** | If the landlord acted in bad faith, up to 2× the deposit on top of the money withheld. A bad-faith failure to document deductions can mean losing the right to keep anything. | The whole deposit, plus up to the deposit amount again in court damages (up to 2× total) | $100 + 3× the amount withheld + fees for bad faith. Bad faith is assumed once the 30 days have passed. |
| **Move-in records** | The landlord must take photos at move-in for tenancies starting July 1, 2025 or later, plus photos before and after repairs that are deducted. Tenants can ask for an inspection before moving out. | Written leases must include a signed inventory and condition record. Photos aren't required. | Not required by state law (no checklist, condition report or photos) |

**Main differences:**
- **Cap:** California is the strictest, Nevada allows up to 3 months, and Texas has no limit.
- **Deadline:** California's 21 days is the shortest. Nevada and Texas both allow 30 days.
- **Penalties:** Texas has the steepest. Because bad faith is assumed once 30 days pass, a late refund there gets expensive fast.
- **Paperwork:** California asks the most, Nevada needs a signed condition record, and Texas requires none. Keeping your own records is still a good idea in every state.

**Sources:**
- **California:** [PropInspect guide](https://propinspect.net/security-deposit-laws/california), last reviewed August 28, 2026. Law: [Cal. Civil Code § 1950.5](https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&sectionNum=1950.5)
- **Nevada:** [PropInspect guide](https://propinspect.net/security-deposit-laws/nevada), last reviewed October 12, 2026. Law: [NRS § 118A.242](https://www.leg.state.nv.us/NRS/NRS-118A.html#NRS118ASec242)
- **Texas:** [PropInspect guide](https://propinspect.net/security-deposit-laws/texas), last reviewed August 28, 2026. Law: [Tex. Prop. Code §§ 92.101–92.111](https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/PR/htm/PR.92.htm)

This is general information, not legal advice. Deposit laws change, and some cities have their own rules. For a specific dispute, talk to a local landlord-tenant attorney or a tenant rights group. In Nevada, the Civil Law Self-Help Center can also help.